  "why_the_page_hash_is_not_the_change_signal": {
    "finding": "Two fetches four minutes apart returned DIFFERENT bytes and an IDENTICAL SAC table. The page is Drupal-rendered and embeds a fresh form_build_id and fresh bootstrap tab element ids on every render; 76 diff lines, none of them inside a SAC table.",
    "measured": {
      "earlier_copy_sha256": "2cf0aa13e9892ca66d6b222907b85150285c4f064507c59795c2ca32adf9605f",
      "pinned_copy_sha256": "e063503cee9c623befc3b8688846aa33591ec3bc44495dd5fbb3d6eec4e8d931",
      "extraction_identical_across_both": true
    },
    "consequence": "The extraction hash below is the change signal and the page hash is not — the exact inverse of the Legion pin, where the served ETag IS the SHA-256 of the body and the document's own info.version is the useless signal. The page hash still earns its place: it identifies the copy that was read, which is a different claim from 'nothing changed'.",
    "how_to_recheck": "Re-fetch the url, re-run the extraction described under `extraction`, and compare `extraction.sha256`. A changed extraction hash means the allocation list moved and the fixtures must be re-checked against it."
  },
